 the corresponding phase space.\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n-5x+2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/07/077f785b498c69dfe131ca766df7bad71600e4c7ac28c5a8013603bc56e3be10.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Nullclines of a System of Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc499e49-8bf9-5a9a-aa98-9f42b95ee211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391040} {:answer/id #uuid "57ad1ccc-ffb5-5ea9-98f0-c37832c17e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391041} {:answer/id #uuid "4b78ad5d-e1cb-50b8-9011-e4ea2a270235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391042} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b7e04e-f150-5a0a-b56b-f06f673923b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b78ad5d-e1cb-50b8-9011-e4ea2a270235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391042} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbc060aa-6832-5d50-951d-180706eee4ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcd393be-3f3a-5d54-b56c-765a247c93c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391045} {:answer/id #uuid "183836bd-4e06-5498-b5e5-9271bdc51698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391046} {:answer/id #uuid "35fcaa94-30df-5985-a1d8-53fde28177f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391047} {:answer/id #uuid "e357655c-0a16-56e0-819e-312ae458e498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35fcaa94-30df-5985-a1d8-53fde28177f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391047} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f45eed4-80c2-5997-82a1-8910e993b93e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad06d77e-d045-5115-beff-bb5f67391033" :question/math-academy-id "q-332662" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n-4x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=4x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/e1/e1d157e7c921aa6fd2dd5449934baf0258dc5a17fc9f6e6db672a783d59553ca.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c7bc7d-0232-5fe1-a6d4-6f9a22440550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c483bbf926df156b7f85fbedcbb6ab18571ff4f544416cd8ea554b70b59a589.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391051} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4028ce-9f71-5bd4-ad6b-47272c4ba666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/27c6bf8d11d3de50beac351f88104df58cac31053eb6ecfc3a35ce77ccd8600b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391052} {:answer/id #uuid "f48f6682-16a0-5a4e-b948-d2adbb5ee402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01d93cb2e68123b061b057ce30b94bcce44401b2d0fc0c566b3c33478c07fb92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391053} {:answer/id #uuid "4d70c978-6c43-538a-a72f-3e3d73db9a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391054} {:answer/id #uuid "e571bbe0-b813-51a0-a84b-7bcbc9e1fb37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/389d3cfc92ece3a63300767f92c34c11dccec981ac43f3f66a2ced02906f8a73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d70c978-6c43-538a-a72f-3e3d73db9a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391054} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f7e5e5-ba46-5c75-bde7-3c2b00f02f16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f7b6c7b-9bbd-58e1-9f4f-ab6d8faeb7f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332683" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cec0e48f-9fbb-5ada-b7fd-0d389d1b7438" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Decoupled Linear System of First-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e380f8b-d71f-55a2-ab1b-39f34cd89e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391058} {:answer/id #uuid "0f9827f0-71cf-5847-85bc-980bee16161f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391059} {:answer/id #uuid "ca29bc6f-4907-5a9b-91bf-39c3f6d764fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391060} {:answer/id #uuid "b312d8e4-025a-5e6d-99d6-bb6fe41ba80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391061} {:answer/id #uuid "755f8ee4-1bac-5f8b-adc2-777c045fa529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b312d8e4-025a-5e6d-99d6-bb6fe41ba80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391061} :answer-field/id #uuid "6964b2cb-9fd8-5726-b5f5-384b2f89e701" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5620c47b-4e97-5ced-9381-5d5cc172acfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-332709" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007e1a63-77b7-5eff-8905-bffd2a2a8d59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9fb1f3c545855e7bdfe591574a9069e69bdcc0cc750e5fc4f5aa3eccfdc7ea5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391065} {:answer/id #uuid "b3aa6c52-d997-5f86-9c55-1e2a11e1e45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/eceeabfeb428687bb9f630fe3578937c3fd4fb910605dc25daf56ad5ce18a68a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391066} {:answer/id #uuid "014409ab-717d-51b8-a1b0-0a3eaabb3c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/64/64c0ac27236c4bfae452572e6b5b4c1d6184c45ebac9149ca4fc442d7533bb51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391067} {:answer/id #uuid "ec30175e-4dad-5eac-9ff8-b58cc3249ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9ff104bc583ec025ede6de68f7ea546dea99c914153b8e2325c6f2f9188d0a9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391068} {:answer/id #uuid "bde5fb9a-33eb-5722-aa37-4cab11937c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde5fb9a-33eb-5722-aa37-4cab11937c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391069} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b3e8fa4-883f-5cc4-809d-2815c7f4d579" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a71e1e9-c69e-580f-aa68-2f882f086377" :question/math-academy-id "q-332751" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/96/96801605aa5d777eb18c13ded1f7b7e59ae74968b210da3b7a26f339a1c9f673.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{7t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{7t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{3t})^{′}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}}{3{c}_{1}{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/20/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca890a8e-9ddd-51f7-9784-c9c1f020adef" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Laplace Transform to a System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbdb0346-df6b-54c5-9f2b-1a51f86464b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{8}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391072} {:answer/id #uuid "abad33f8-c615-55cd-be7e-a876cf8b798d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{6}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391073} {:answer/id #uuid "93b0be01-b2d1-52a7-a7b2-eab077553a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391074} {:answer/id #uuid "bcafc412-1624-5229-a866-bdc0ecb4644e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{4}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb0346-df6b-54c5-9f2b-1a51f86464b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{8}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391072} :answer-field/id #uuid "02170356-555d-5391-844a-aa4a685bc899" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d308951-21e4-5c21-9ef4-c63b86017bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{2}(s)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391077} {:answer/id #uuid "30854c60-4af3-51dd-9745-64d42226705b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{X}_{2}(s)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391078} {:answer/id #uuid "0484d010-db77-5049-9ad7-541adf72d481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{X}_{2}(s)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391079} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c3c734-8f49-5762-882f-f734b8ee9d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{2}(s)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30854c60-4af3-51dd-9745-64d42226705b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{X}_{2}(s)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391078} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bfe627c-2e52-5629-8187-3e04a818bfa9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67c13fb5-3143-5582-b4e2-b3c94c89f6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391082} {:answer/id #uuid "06a18db9-6d16-5dd4-af44-42485526ba3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391083} {:answer/id #uuid "47a8ac13-7622-5a11-bdcf-90a606f7164e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391084} {:answer/id #uuid "7594e37a-2a99-5457-accb-611a2b85e238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06a18db9-6d16-5dd4-af44-42485526ba3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391083} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6a81987-3f3f-5953-8eba-950e37790f05" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b3114e0-fee2-509a-aff6-396fcfbc6f47" :question/math-academy-id "q-332814" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+4{t}^{2}+2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)-4,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)={{field-1}}+\\frac{2}{s} \\\\ {{field-2}}=-2{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)-{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)+4{t}^{2}+2} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{-2{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)-4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}+4L{{t}^{2}}+2L{1} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L{{x}_{1}(t)}+2L{{x}_{2}(t)}-4L{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,L{{t}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\n$$\n\n we can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{8}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3=-2{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)-\\frac{4}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca890a8e-9ddd-51f7-9784-c9c1f020adef" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Laplace Transform to a System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4a3129f-60db-5bb1-9402-1caab26f253e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{2}(s)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391088} {:answer/id #uuid "d725fa79-ce91-56b1-b811-df62d4e99f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{X}_{2}(s)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391089} {:answer/id #uuid "0cca240b-f67e-5644-a2a9-2880ae887120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{2}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391090} {:answer/id #uuid "30b134f9-e5ed-5c56-b12a-47b3f6096ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{X}_{2}(s)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d725fa79-ce91-56b1-b811-df62d4e99f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{X}_{2}(s)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391089} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa16e7de-ab9f-51c2-a861-32c430e88f9a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2cd568e-aff0-550f-ae94-39ec8fc48b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391093} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7d564e-7efd-5645-8cd3-95ccf2331e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391094} {:answer/id #uuid "0a02e1d2-6500-5f9b-9a54-da78f763a140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391095} {:answer/id #uuid "4253a6c7-471a-5724-842c-30b7c93fe5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b7d564e-7efd-5645-8cd3-95ccf2331e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391094} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44f68a1-957a-5844-be7b-c61600113732" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c168c67-ffe1-50bf-8923-4c94835c0396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{6}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391098} {:answer/id #uuid "599f6364-bbb2-5d50-9761-11676746e3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{5}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391099} {:answer/id #uuid "4931632e-d8a5-5e99-abdf-6d7cb3329e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{4}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391100} {:answer/id #uuid "d25be132-4720-5af1-9e61-1cba7faa6177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{3}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c168c67-ffe1-50bf-8923-4c94835c0396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{6}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391098} :answer-field/id #uuid "5480d0ef-7f3e-5c29-836b-04b07c0f328d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a065f0e5-b523-5238-8fa5-968a6404de76" :question/math-academy-id "q-332820" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)={{field-1}} \\\\ {{field-2}}=-3{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)+{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)+6} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}+6L{1} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=-3L{{x}_{1}(t)}+2L{{x}_{2}(t)}+L{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the result\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\n$$\n\n we can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{6}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-1=-3{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20557dcd-5576-575d-9801-a4932bfaed7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11546c8b1c69c2f4f14020f3d7fdbfc322b02e9f6661803783ee54ccd97648e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391104} {:answer/id #uuid "d9629f02-41a5-56bb-be5c-27f85295fb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/60/60a82fc6876f894285d41742d6ad62d177aa230136b7cdc440b97b392f6c398d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391105} {:answer/id #uuid "47cb6758-4945-5b68-8ca3-e6e504afaff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/460b3691ace4d67d6fd526a8bb9b244a0d2fb55170ed1aac1f2cb6aa849c72c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391106} {:answer/id #uuid "b487d4d5-b824-5bc4-91d1-efcc8f6424ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b24594a5ab369e6952ffb76ded8a48e4186d457d9108e154cb4fd8f96b1749ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391107} {:answer/id #uuid "6550b2ea-565c-5828-9532-04b7406a0cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1cb187f62a6ebbc96ca31ac3bd5008189c9879ab7f0df3f94e83a2e36c465d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47cb6758-4945-5b68-8ca3-e6e504afaff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/460b3691ace4d67d6fd526a8bb9b244a0d2fb55170ed1aac1f2cb6aa849c72c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391106} :answer-field/id #uuid "aed41a04-9bfb-5cce-98a4-628bcc71e7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4df72b41-05b7-51ad-9387-de772f46c676" :question/math-academy-id "q-332835" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6351966b8cc79941da7c5cd1af6d590a20d62aaf72204fce96137b8676047f1.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (upward and slightly to the left) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](images/56/56a39f156ab7cf44471d2984765e2b13fc46b4d1ec24805b92385d4d9b118da6.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues going upward but then turns to the right.\n\n![](images/29/297c4c1891788880cb43bb8b70126ead37c75dd0942d63ff79c12f6b3edd676d.png)\n\n  \n\n- After that, the trajectory goes to the right and slightly upward to infinity.\n\n![](images/80/80f1cb1edcbf35c4a3b87c0954a9302729f066d8dadf6205de7231343ab6e998.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f927a4-1b64-540f-bb7c-e15e61482db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/80d758f23c0962d0611fca95f5ce5e7654346f133ceb7cf1275aaf3071ed1838.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391111} {:answer/id #uuid "fda175e8-b02e-5fd6-b042-504a1948f51a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0f/0f513a1fb254df7d6a6aaaa13632a0312b5483b622bcc83a154b4e80bd392a42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391112} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e13b20-d127-5c45-9a52-5a283c7618b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e3/e3b4b7001ce149759e4356b594dace06641e815860d33a6e2a2b3143078b58fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391113} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1c6572-7c67-5521-8482-c910338dc838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c1/c1517d81fb17366779bf77a0608f5f8d691f302d009d0f677a6ca0c6c02cd527.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391114} {:answer/id #uuid "03b7217f-4a6f-50f5-982b-e1649d39aa5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f49c17becabf4088e886ddfc8e086c81184d94e6637c44458205c47410be1a3d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65f927a4-1b64-540f-bb7c-e15e61482db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/80d758f23c0962d0611fca95f5ce5e7654346f133ceb7cf1275aaf3071ed1838.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391111} :answer-field/id #uuid "829923eb-1895-5bc9-8cd1-365ba2040166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9ff10b6-480b-50d2-b813-5532190d7591" :question/math-academy-id "q-332837" :question/problem "![](images/97/9703f99ca670b5924bf668fc77bcc09d8be652487259b0ea8753fc38c85d7013.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (downward and to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](images/7f/7f98b4ad206556ac8a7c8eae16d216af9d91a375907e3fd058247e70195e1669.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues going to the right and slightly upward to infinity.\n\n![](images/3b/3ba48b118f4ed24dcf6866579fae201af0b36252e537ab99ac2b87e08a889e15.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "329b39c3-fb14-58dd-92bb-ab24fd06dd48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/934a6376a6a550d7f63cd0afa80421f9a44e44f7dfafef800247acdc39105824.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391118} {:answer/id #uuid "684fdf7d-0604-5330-aa31-6a0c196a2d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0b/0bda142f71e111c2e365256db32f5d7ca14fe55a4092374883862def1e2670c8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391119} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7e8f75-6b4f-5d84-9eae-4b4a6840c618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c7/c7e6753bedde2aa38cb3c2deabc24dcf807d96526f754862b5094c02a8e64a29.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391120} {:answer/id #uuid "af4e357a-4776-5104-9547-7efb89ddee63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f1b08eeefa04416ab881c406ff68ed91cd2734f8c89b47974437955abf142a80.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391121} {:answer/id #uuid "edc13a13-167b-5b9f-b139-c8d86cf61c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/067cf931d6ebf881c4bd498ac0032bea74169e26df4f8ee54938256632d5fadc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "329b39c3-fb14-58dd-92bb-ab24fd06dd48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/934a6376a6a550d7f63cd0afa80421f9a44e44f7dfafef800247acdc39105824.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391118} :answer-field/id #uuid "b84e3c91-85df-570f-9dc1-8c1f3000fe85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04858659-8500-5f92-bd60-b049f6b940c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332840" :question/problem "![](images/61/61e0a98fa1d8946c82b93dca1ab7a8df80e2c3fe376e6acd9b6c8e8d40780038.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (to the left and slightly upward) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](images/85/850d938e2a00d7775c20c00c7a904bd2f5d41d619c76f48fe701d187c62b11b9.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues to the left, turning slightly downward.\n\n![](images/30/30d303651aa2c2c10e4d57a32e5113a1d3b58f9a96b98fe8276a303675b494f6.png)\n\n- The trajectory continues to the left and down. It keeps going to infinity.\n\n![](images/f7/f75bcf11c3e23d319b2cac2d952b28d10f24a07205a374bdf0fd1f741c2a1c01.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99d366cd-e032-513e-afad-5fd6fb04cd37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/be/bea7fb72b9847f4862be6f7b17f1bbba6e6ea3891861cadf1dea79521ed80ec0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391125} {:answer/id #uuid "3737078d-7556-51ee-8384-5389dc56646c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f038e336ebc3dd016ffcd2a56318e0474976335e4295f1ed3260dcf55ea23f53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391126} {:answer/id #uuid "0058a909-4714-53aa-bb21-7cc85a85d8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ab/abcaeea2568c1f5f9969e855d2394bbb16fbc5a3589430f562ae3ba745ede675.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391127} {:answer/id #uuid "df56f9d8-3bda-5b55-a0d2-39e4fbe9fd50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b12e7c37f388f69649997e2f0ea520c99c30e6d5b996aee408570e03792d1d0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391128} {:answer/id #uuid "107fb89c-c3ef-5fda-adc6-6102b5fdbfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/9213923ffccce206fd161352cbc9ee209763018e00565c6fa948dcad27d50f35.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0058a909-4714-53aa-bb21-7cc85a85d8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ab/abcaeea2568c1f5f9969e855d2394bbb16fbc5a3589430f562ae3ba745ede675.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391127} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31a3a63-654b-5892-9834-73484b64d333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fefce015-19ce-5065-9c7a-242b11884ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332863" :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee9e024a0299df3fa1b13c56a53217d638a8ace90148303aa14e34061ad44180.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, going along with the flow (upward and slightly to the right), and crossing the x-nullcline where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](images/6a/6aa850c4b0fb7cde00bed079de3cde5c2fcfa22c5c2bdae40b43ddb099f27cc7.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory goes to the right and slightly upward to infinity.\n\n![](images/05/052e7aa9e5dbf874536d9f32268c5d28114067128878e5734beb4a0568ec2a94.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf35dfa0-9962-5b80-b4b5-43589a527a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa6cf07dc92efbd6eca770d4c9af2aa3eaa312d585e2a44169967e346a236198.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391132} {:answer/id #uuid "90bb54c9-8830-5249-af87-9fe394fccae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/81/813739fde6ecbd8b97d37cd500f104fbd0aafd6c3247e38569ed1b2e16d7d351.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391133} {:answer/id #uuid "d15cd470-f9e9-53b8-8eb0-f8f6f06fecbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c3abb4df91d6f9c3da4eb5394eea9a73df8eed6a782ed0eccede9e946e5836e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391134} {:answer/id #uuid "596df1d3-4c59-577e-837d-a9c34a04092f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/41a653adc61eec8ab01704b2171e14c8dd0454880db7c643929142f7544a23c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391135} {:answer/id #uuid "c1eee7e2-7247-5ea9-85e2-680b08f7385e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d49ef93ba4beb20d33346f90cab0d2b0c6cd2833b10a3390facfdf2d070e0c18.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d15cd470-f9e9-53b8-8eb0-f8f6f06fecbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c3abb4df91d6f9c3da4eb5394eea9a73df8eed6a782ed0eccede9e946e5836e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391134} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15fcabe-7e02-5c52-a4e7-650c736a8a8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8ff40f4-1d1b-5478-9383-2934565c286e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332873" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9103a8e40d3040b711d0bcfc8cf759d6515ba7d0ae060239d203940e4b09daf.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (slightly downward and to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](images/14/146fd1dd846803a8ee5684756a1ddf92b4236b49ad3082bd2397b07eb30671aa.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues to the right and gradually turns upward.\n\n![](images/cf/cf3b24d0c6249d7b60803ea62a06c160b987b2892d4e56f91f0001d276ec4838.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory keeps going to the right and slightly upward to infinity.\n\n![](images/58/58cefcb6d77335a6dd54aba7bdffdb19024fd8c6aab0659389097ba1ced6d6a0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc017f0-fa0b-53d8-b90f-a0d020af9d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e2/e265cd75cfd7cd4178a6d222d86043d97c816d36286b516898d8ff0b0e6d208c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391138} {:answer/id #uuid "266d074e-ce24-5bf4-988e-95150096a2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/5852fdce770beeaa73192b4487c445a5b6cdd857a01b08465c60c1ffeeba9aed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391139} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ca23fb-815f-5016-8412-6468504a8837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6d/6daec64ca18c09907c43f10f5f2e1b46f693782bddcccb8fccd0240534120100.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391140} {:answer/id #uuid "0662e1c8-a634-569b-ae58-c7f5dccb33ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/dffc5c6e0d736e34aae32e10158a1e9ea5071562cd50cc630fe6b82740dfe439.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391141} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cbcbee-4126-5a07-ad49-6ed0503afbbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9f32c0e475c4973fed376a2392bad1b159123da933c6defb7145cc6ab6ce2634.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0662e1c8-a634-569b-ae58-c7f5dccb33ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/dffc5c6e0d736e34aae32e10158a1e9ea5071562cd50cc630fe6b82740dfe439.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391141} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c010e65-847f-51f1-966d-9f1ee42eecc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c356d0ae-4461-596b-a446-f5773fdb52cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-332920" :question/problem "![](images/14/14ed16cc149ae777e195aa59e39cf809e939032a925e7acbd88cd685af99dd0e.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (straight to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](images/0d/0d010be8bad72bf4ebac4dbe2d325f1a1f194d78c676e2a62306b5c7d2af3c66.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues going to the right  but then starts turning downward.\n\n![](images/51/5118424696b9602f10bf116c3a292e2fb58ed2f9acd2a9507888ab92a9b8986b.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to move rightward and bend downward, converging to the origin.\n\n![](images/92/92a58573efafd7155cf02faf52e27ab968d9161ad448530b45673fd83a61fde1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c43c9d-da3b-5cc5-aff8-5e064531ce24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391145} {:answer/id #uuid "9f671a2c-e2b8-5d20-b384-708b60c2aa83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391146} {:answer/id #uuid "abeb4eea-879b-549d-b142-846725e874b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391147} {:answer/id #uuid "91d3b27d-c9ac-5477-8d36-4c208bef7a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391148} {:answer/id #uuid "b4874601-5bf2-5413-9532-581c0bfd893b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abeb4eea-879b-549d-b142-846725e874b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391147} :answer-field/id #uuid "6856b1da-9b23-517a-a9e0-8ccdcf647ba0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecd7ba84-c62d-5665-b104-2afa83226b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333120" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}+{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-2-(2)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-2-4 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6095321f-3c3a-5564-bd76-8ac789465110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/635e8687fbccc7612f692ec1a1abaa9802bafe9c3773e4b0c058a18813e7249e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391152} {:answer/id #uuid "9f24dea2-7b89-5ce9-93de-ca7b97049c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a5/a5ae215022638430d0c89dddd97c01ae7598b6251bd013ef48ca9771029b8cd6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391153} {:answer/id #uuid "f70a8ce1-5b6e-5473-90f3-bf87bae67685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9816edf7fb99b759361abc4333f3ed0d3676d2c77db2210367466b66837bca8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391154} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcceae6-4fa4-5965-9386-71fc659c0936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391155} {:answer/id #uuid "65324022-a078-5bdb-b4aa-e0381dbb7e37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4f613e414cdb619a9b09f25a47b7e4cad953440c40d91f876322d7e1cb59b8c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fcceae6-4fa4-5965-9386-71fc659c0936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391155} :answer-field/id #uuid "41bd2e0d-7517-5053-bcbd-08e7df5307b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34a4a204-b6f5-559d-8b71-d224e42d358b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333177" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{9t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/99/99d6e9894c7117c5684e17702540d0eb469b6e824381cf2263c843f8f55ee8d8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{9t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{9{c}_{1}{e}^{9t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{9{c}_{1}}{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/c4/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6009f5a2-68a9-5943-8731-39f000ed0199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e8/e8e9a97ed196b5677e3c2ea6be32f4a1ab04f7721b3d9c41e4db284d69e6805a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391159} {:answer/id #uuid "1b93024f-8207-52ad-845f-920de06ed5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/85cb5c01c72b8b903cc0b5fc8c593c0b6e96550380e6bc7af55d5db5b5f40d4e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391160} {:answer/id #uuid "4a78d0da-5220-5127-8319-73b4acac217f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b414b76f1fe20b5238e75281e57bb851c8e67777e225d7610631dab1b4921b82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391161} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cd3807-c498-5cde-99b9-07cd5a9bfab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d19a6d30c12f5d966578cd950db1f16a9bc309f3a24a8e2480e52edca1281f75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391162} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaa09e6-0c8b-54b3-b59e-baf355087c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c70e2c185d054b8cc62411736ecec2e60a716d265819a82a171076d999ef4e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6009f5a2-68a9-5943-8731-39f000ed0199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e8/e8e9a97ed196b5677e3c2ea6be32f4a1ab04f7721b3d9c41e4db284d69e6805a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391159} :answer-field/id #uuid "69405513-1dbb-597a-8b25-1607c72536b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "619ed97d-e84b-53da-afc0-cbcda9b4c479" :question/math-academy-id "q-333193" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/3f/3fdbeba46a0f4640e909b0ba489c1aec1fa87a6aa7740f19d08f274b7351d11b.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-3{c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/41/4193300af5a8950ce70e2c469812424235665faac279dac0c89289a0307ab46a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b653a6f8-189a-5b30-8676-00bdf69976b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391166} {:answer/id #uuid "76824f50-2011-5389-8865-1c3c99710ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391167} {:answer/id #uuid "42384210-dd84-51db-86c7-8af0a48421c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b653a6f8-189a-5b30-8676-00bdf69976b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391166} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec537181-46cd-5656-9b07-265e8db0cda5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94378a66-51f5-59e7-b62e-7c7f5786d523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391170} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8f7574-660b-5b66-a3ff-ee866cfa988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94378a66-51f5-59e7-b62e-7c7f5786d523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391170} :answer-field/id #uuid "04770685-b678-5602-b414-10a8f3bc9c53" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b37844d-8d08-5cd8-91cb-9d3bc83a76ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391173} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac827e7-003d-5e37-9e57-e8729f512cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b37844d-8d08-5cd8-91cb-9d3bc83a76ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391173} :answer-field/id #uuid "09f6e2ad-0744-5cb5-b9d9-a865a67a877a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1c82443-160c-5ecb-b7e3-f0a7c6455443" :question/math-academy-id "q-333224" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/06/062b58a307f6d40c7be59d527cc01d7d301bd3f0115a0074ba0f3dca54412425.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461def2d-f65b-5702-81dc-9c688ad9792a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391177} {:answer/id #uuid "64590024-0b5d-5509-b3ed-cec5555ef505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391178} {:answer/id #uuid "e9097811-7f04-5636-ac94-08d654db1f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "461def2d-f65b-5702-81dc-9c688ad9792a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391177} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78979b2-0930-5c53-983d-999f23a46873" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5edfc04a-0185-57d1-a0a8-90f14d6bb9d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391181} {:answer/id #uuid "83c65d7a-dd3e-5392-bb3e-959427004b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5edfc04a-0185-57d1-a0a8-90f14d6bb9d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391181} :answer-field/id #uuid "110033c6-9468-57ae-9c4b-2409f3360843" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ad3d999-893c-5b32-86ed-3c76b9777309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391184} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf150cb-9ddf-51fd-a897-b57d640d5664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ad3d999-893c-5b32-86ed-3c76b9777309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391184} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb6a2c1-2961-5d3d-8a0c-af139a57aa82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7685d561-af7c-5963-8ff0-5f1c98ad02e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333227" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b4/b406c864e820819c5ae6f548c7a025a1e1d8197fb785ea5689080034e04210cb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f8e54c-0615-5591-b542-0b782cd89daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391188} {:answer/id #uuid "de1fd91f-8a5e-51f3-8dc2-a58537f9f1b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391189} {:answer/id #uuid "c540887b-e7fe-5d55-972c-cf0a8340ba96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,0,4⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391190} {:answer/id #uuid "796aa680-c468-5852-b522-12b6bd2cf262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩=⟨-3,0,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391191} {:answer/id #uuid "eee5c6a8-ae14-524b-a64c-c15d96be1896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,0,4⟩=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03f8e54c-0615-5591-b542-0b782cd89daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391188} :answer-field/id #uuid "54786791-8d86-5133-85dc-e9b4fbb89dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa8c997f-15c5-5b2a-b1e2-bd26aa6233d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-33324" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-t \\\\ y=4+3t \\\\ z=-6+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-t \\\\ y=4+3t \\\\ z=-6+5t\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,4,-6⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-1,3,5⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-1,3,5⟩.$ So, a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-1,3,5⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a Decoupled System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa084511-df9a-52dd-9bd1-276adcecff68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bffb2f815cc5a7db0c521054006aa834b17071f5dd33a97fe977e5d118b771be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391195} {:answer/id #uuid "0b7dced2-671c-54c6-aac9-f3bd84260b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391196} {:answer/id #uuid "8e213fcc-e76e-5621-a3cb-ee3aa7c30e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391197} {:answer/id #uuid "9376d246-4191-572d-b767-91ed1b3613d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14ae87fe329b09385dc0b4fa3238b6acfe428138bfdd438d99dd22cc12f94f9a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391198} {:answer/id #uuid "5e47e0eb-cc6a-5323-ae2c-7719ad7d9671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b7dced2-671c-54c6-aac9-f3bd84260b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391196} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af1b5dc-48bc-57ad-a9be-047a4bcca023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659c7156-a7e5-547d-940d-48b6db396c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333271" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a Decoupled System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2e15d48-bf85-5d42-ba3e-1338107759f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f975014051d49d3a3fbbba048c5def4ad4f6f0e88e65304fdb9bba6666b9a2a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391202} {:answer/id #uuid "65ca2087-5574-543f-97e1-261c3be98f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/440d53841ecc6606923528349393c30e201a125ca659de7f95a4749c5bdc58a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391203} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd311f8-aa4b-592d-b8b0-22647607745a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a2/a27e83035006f198d454498aafe91624e0576ab43993bde970faf426a9e4cef2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391204} {:answer/id #uuid "8c888fa2-840a-5792-95ed-4a0909a9983c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391205} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb156e0-1fde-5f1d-96b9-181d82648c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bd311f8-aa4b-592d-b8b0-22647607745a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a2/a27e83035006f198d454498aafe91624e0576ab43993bde970faf426a9e4cef2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391204} :answer-field/id #uuid "8084df77-4e0e-50aa-ac58-6caddb32bf9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e28f179-f86f-51da-b231-a7cf6df4b435" :question/math-academy-id "q-333290" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/a2/a27e83035006f198d454498aafe91624e0576ab43993bde970faf426a9e4cef2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822ac31f-fd59-5c24-bd6a-a144699796af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220403} {:answer/id #uuid "b7718c17-8459-5fd0-97e2-8b47804c7caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220404} {:answer/id #uuid "e530f5de-7137-569f-b10a-053e324c9cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220405} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b49f0b-9676-55b0-b673-8bf536b71cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220406} {:answer/id #uuid "3563a3f9-53d2-5c0f-94f3-70ffefcfa11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220407}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2326d025-2caa-5e2b-b5a5-5b29e6091c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220402}] :question/id #uuid "8eda104b-fb43-5560-b213-0b2f03bde928" :question/math-academy-id "q-333360" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) - y(t) - 1$\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11849504-966c-58fa-863a-9d925407d3c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391208} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2a069c-609e-583c-978a-6c288d9af47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11849504-966c-58fa-863a-9d925407d3c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391208} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b272f6-637b-5ea0-8700-e5f7d86f7f6a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391207} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "512dbb1c-ad34-53bb-b6aa-d476941e80d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391211} {:answer/id #uuid "aaed2bc6-a9a6-5220-82fe-401940c11d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "512dbb1c-ad34-53bb-b6aa-d476941e80d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391211} :answer-field/id #uuid "42fb6754-3f60-584f-8e8c-64f87a1b9091" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391210} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848ba988-ee07-5611-b978-c788c6c35a5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391214} {:answer/id #uuid "99d0bffe-aea4-5f88-a834-76108f8dae94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391215} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cbdd8d-c657-558a-b8d1-c64ef71723c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391216} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca3db64-7ada-5db5-b594-508ba910d0c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4cbdd8d-c657-558a-b8d1-c64ef71723c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391216} :answer-field/id #uuid "a961eff4-a4cc-5f40-8e2c-1194d07931d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ccce0b3-5f04-5d5a-9e2d-ebcf2aa205bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333517" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "777fae40-2377-5e63-89f6-5c857beba2b2" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying an Isolated Critical Point of a Nonlinear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9244b400-42ab-58df-b8b0-a3bbc7cba75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391219} {:answer/id #uuid "0daed90d-a153-582e-90da-175afd8fd964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391220} {:answer/id #uuid "602df48b-efc0-58a3-a4e8-f8eafc30ceaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9244b400-42ab-58df-b8b0-a3bbc7cba75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391219} :answer-field/id #uuid "2afcd599-57e3-5297-b831-71b01faf8aa5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391218} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2c67e4d-8b9b-5f92-a771-1827d4638f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391223} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd6bf2a-d1b6-56e4-984f-6951a8cdadff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cd6bf2a-d1b6-56e4-984f-6951a8cdadff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391224} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4eec63b-b845-54c1-bfe7-24f2abb290f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e06a687f-dc13-5f88-9f73-09d6b8609d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-333532" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x(t))+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=1-{x}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 1 \\\\ -2(1) & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda \\cdot (-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1750bded-03a2-535d-9322-af370f69f5d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391227} {:answer/id #uuid "7b95026e-9297-5fe5-b515-53db91622691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b95026e-9297-5fe5-b515-53db91622691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391228} :answer-field/id #uuid "23101e77-153a-53ab-8eaa-fb91f151bfef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391226} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4552e55a-71fb-5355-a8ff-c44fe89b0c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391230} {:answer/id #uuid "79852ea3-d668-55cf-ae57-0e55042a72c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4552e55a-71fb-5355-a8ff-c44fe89b0c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391230} :answer-field/id #uuid "5874e815-f2f7-5127-b199-5dfa1e8dd28e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04b55cbe-b4be-543b-bbe4-7f5a1dc7e192" :question/math-academy-id "q-333614" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c1/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3550c5b-1a8d-5dcc-94d9-d43ecbc42265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391234} {:answer/id #uuid "df63b9f7-a38a-5edb-993f-36ceedd86317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df63b9f7-a38a-5edb-993f-36ceedd86317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391235} :answer-field/id #uuid "c76521b0-e06a-5475-a7d5-6e2974a8f112" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391233} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5312169-7d7a-54df-9140-8a7bb5968066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391237} {:answer/id #uuid "950b0d68-2c7b-597b-b535-d524113f3c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "950b0d68-2c7b-597b-b535-d524113f3c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391238} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbadf579-62d3-5b73-9773-8e20894a11ba" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391236} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d613f505-1a04-57a4-949e-1aebd360a3de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391240} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0e1ce6-267e-5fd4-84d6-3eac77237c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391241} {:answer/id #uuid "75d86f9f-3608-5ff1-bb40-eff9c641175e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391242} {:answer/id #uuid "7317d9a1-da8b-52e0-9556-0183aed9099f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d613f505-1a04-57a4-949e-1aebd360a3de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391240} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a9b4d0-45cb-542f-8d79-a61ac6b9c372" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391239} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9ca126e-4488-59a9-afb7-e069ca2b8039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391245} {:answer/id #uuid "8019d76c-7e55-5f9e-aeaf-ce134837dc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391246} {:answer/id #uuid "83dc74d9-67b1-531a-a5b9-359c88d95b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83dc74d9-67b1-531a-a5b9-359c88d95b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391247} :answer-field/id #uuid "e05694af-fcc2-57e8-a41a-79ef3c0578f2" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d111108f-9aed-5eb7-8811-2065601ee640" :question/math-academy-id "q-333640" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$\n$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/0d/0d9e032bfffa68f487347d6c169999e313247e89ef7de3fae28edb216dcf718f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b0634b8-f6ab-5ea4-b71a-8ff79c2910ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391249} {:answer/id #uuid "5856e40c-0770-51af-9c03-743a46d8596e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391250} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1a4796-e97f-5429-9cff-49ce80190e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391251} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8f23ef-ea1d-51ba-a4c9-2d39a0d74b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d8f23ef-ea1d-51ba-a4c9-2d39a0d74b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391252} :answer-field/id #uuid "c05b5222-3879-5657-af29-d4fe6f0070f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391248} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7d4ce44-9e69-52f2-9caa-64e6d8f34a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391254} {:answer/id #uuid "22c29fa9-881d-5b05-8f81-c4fd49e6cd53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7d4ce44-9e69-52f2-9caa-64e6d8f34a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391254} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec1f102-d858-549e-95e7-119125b750c1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff6701bd-a4f7-52e4-90bd-ddcbd4815a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391257} {:answer/id #uuid "57f9df6d-c566-5125-a5ee-dfefdfea7aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff6701bd-a4f7-52e4-90bd-ddcbd4815a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391257} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ef32066-7efb-5518-b08e-5b73f307a698" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391256} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c1d23b5-f76a-5cfb-b2b7-024a619b5bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391260} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ef6ad1-d41a-592f-9f2a-dafc9e0ed0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391261} {:answer/id #uuid "29fe338f-9f99-59e0-b948-9af05832bed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c1d23b5-f76a-5cfb-b2b7-024a619b5bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391260} :answer-field/id #uuid "244f6b3b-3578-574d-94fd-0dcddcc4492b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f735f61a-0791-5bf2-b5e1-a997e0f18859" :question/math-academy-id "q-333646" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$\n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/63/6337a670b6fa73c4c21759b600269cb83d808a1c13026c90e2d02519fa31cd67.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a91b22-3c2e-591c-95dd-7d31a5689a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391265} {:answer/id #uuid "c3746fa1-fc10-5bb5-88e6-d4d1facc4dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02a91b22-3c2e-591c-95dd-7d31a5689a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391265} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e7eaec-059c-5d14-9f8c-61c1f07a19ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0901b7b-91df-5d1e-af60-9e839083f14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391268} {:answer/id #uuid "37c33283-6fbe-5f50-a58a-dfc0bc8c628a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0901b7b-91df-5d1e-af60-9e839083f14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391268} :answer-field/id #uuid "32217f1b-d451-5dd0-8e1b-fafec5464418" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13d1d15c-2bcc-581d-b50d-d0762931b97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333678" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =-1$ and it's negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92efcce675a436ad349ca6139cb12cda88fbdd5bdeaf2a5f1e755e7ba1f6d037.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Properties of a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e315641f-7048-5d22-999e-3be3cfc5af5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391272} {:answer/id #uuid "0233e06e-a302-51e4-b083-fe0aaa4725aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391273} {:answer/id #uuid "be3009e6-e7cf-57fb-8c1f-a885361311eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391274} {:answer/id #uuid "cf515a09-9dba-5d02-ae42-355ff706ce78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e315641f-7048-5d22-999e-3be3cfc5af5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391272} :answer-field/id #uuid "85305209-f127-50f4-8fb6-558c6f7e40e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391271} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a700d19b-464e-5807-9333-12ea7e2f2e4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391277} {:answer/id #uuid "9667e6c0-3577-5729-b0b1-c1fd903f6823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}$ is a solution of the system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391278} {:answer/id #uuid "c43f1625-204a-5fc4-acbc-a60997cdf542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}$ is differentiable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391279} {:answer/id #uuid "27100933-11fb-5602-87aa-f18a23a2da08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9667e6c0-3577-5729-b0b1-c1fd903f6823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}$ is a solution of the system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391278} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a119ab6-43f8-5872-ab0c-26e93853cfdf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d630e396-6325-5e09-89df-19e69a52dddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "factoring out $A$ on the left" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391282} {:answer/id #uuid "63c0991b-ad6e-5f6a-98b8-643da1aae492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "transposing the matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391283} {:answer/id #uuid "351118ef-d9b7-5b2c-8103-6248dbbc0401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating again" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391284} {:answer/id #uuid "cafa980d-badb-57fc-a8c3-a2ffc2a64bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ(t)$ to both sides" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d630e396-6325-5e09-89df-19e69a52dddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "factoring out $A$ on the left" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391282} :answer-field/id #uuid "155738af-3e2a-57a9-a635-a3db6456eb1e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49e5968a-1ea4-5510-81b8-9a383e60e6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333719" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A\\,Φ(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $[a,\\,b]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $a$ and $b.$*\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L4}$ and substitution from lines {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{1}\\text{ is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{′}(t) & {x}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{ and }\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=A[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=AΦ(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out }A\\text{ on the left}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f116bab-260d-5a94-9454-883cdffc58b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2}{x-C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391287} {:answer/id #uuid "32f53127-9873-5a47-8afa-f556ed137706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391288} {:answer/id #uuid "039913ea-8d3d-544a-911a-95cae93c1540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{2x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391289} {:answer/id #uuid "61b162e0-5e1f-5b0e-a533-a5eeeabd8cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391290} {:answer/id #uuid "d85f8f7c-bea4-53a7-9749-020982226aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32f53127-9873-5a47-8afa-f556ed137706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391288} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97c3693-73f3-50f9-ab47-9b737fd8c20c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9b2868a-b882-5b68-b22b-3bc42a1c4dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333805" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}y+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=1,\\,b(x)=-\\frac{4}{x},\\,c(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\frac{2}{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=(z+\\frac{2}{x})^{2}-\\frac{4}{x}(z+\\frac{2}{x})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =({z}^{2}+\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})-(\\frac{4z}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & ={z}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-\\frac{2}{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y-\\frac{2}{x} & =-\\frac{1}{x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x+C}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fed07ddb-975d-5a6f-9b33-90b64c27d3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391294} {:answer/id #uuid "a26dae11-7922-5cde-a25b-9e2a34d335e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fcc3957699640731ddae9ccbb98e958882d8bdbb2f2b506112baa3553b03f47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391295} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f8982e-0372-594a-a7c8-2ba5fa1945b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a7/a76320ebfd18b1bc272c5421309a4a98c34abc5437c56c6d40a83a84f80f0157.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391296} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c091f7-e94d-5c27-8029-5ce245439c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dd/dd3667299cd5da8fe5d9450799fbe865e832449ac39427795a5b87cf2786f587.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391297} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c7f702-bdf9-57d9-9b22-fdeeb2287606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f7784ccd8bbfe05ce743e71f92cfbecfa4d4a8b19d0664337d2c67d360b3d3e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fed07ddb-975d-5a6f-9b33-90b64c27d3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391294} :answer-field/id #uuid "458a647e-6339-5a42-879a-e69d51bbf669" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c13a40a3-bc1a-5171-a2d7-22e438148666" :question/math-academy-id "q-333814" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/99/99629c2f73e3a10db2ef139848c93c6a2489d995d3d992b7dfb0332989b4f48f.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc325f8-243b-5130-a053-df4211d94d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391301} {:answer/id #uuid "d13587aa-4415-592d-b84a-ac9148da1da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391302} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d7bfd8-aa3b-5bf2-b12f-bd4b4c6876d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8c699c5e49f42ddf1d57f3686f7fa7c06ca3f941aa8e65f7700f855b0134df13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391303} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ccc559-2cfe-5ecb-9b80-8f02e4bf3705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06b9fd74afcc99a3396aa280b248272904c495d6181e6a5c027c0cd6a30acbfb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391304} {:answer/id #uuid "3919ca1c-1c68-55f4-b720-13f63a99bffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a695d58031c47e43d0aed186e271eff7d3da271ea83ae3c053be011afe9d4707.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13587aa-4415-592d-b84a-ac9148da1da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391302} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e9d729b-0d66-5bca-bdc1-c82610c1e6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ec66512-f01a-54c5-9b43-60952356c9b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-333824" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b3/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/12/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de03094c-f5a7-50bf-9f41-e5a8f23ad238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391308} {:answer/id #uuid "b18dba31-d2ce-5fa5-afc7-3b19b8d18941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18dba31-d2ce-5fa5-afc7-3b19b8d18941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391309} :answer-field/id #uuid "49882bcd-1ee1-52be-90bc-ec435809b713" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391307} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cd908bd-d2b1-5cd2-88d1-1a6a96866587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391311} {:answer/id #uuid "edb7ee60-60d1-59d0-b737-8b9f2ccfb756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cd908bd-d2b1-5cd2-88d1-1a6a96866587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391311} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e98f621-0812-5486-a6ec-f9e9bc134950" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391310} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aff834c0-ead9-5763-bbef-15bcddb83098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391314} {:answer/id #uuid "8a22e18a-616e-5ede-b34a-500b508c1616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391315} {:answer/id #uuid "be7b6578-72f9-57a4-b506-ecf1d9c3f1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391316} {:answer/id #uuid "82dc805f-3bec-5d37-95ae-d62211dffd9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aff834c0-ead9-5763-bbef-15bcddb83098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391314} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd34adb-688d-50aa-a1bb-5f912809ee74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81a7dc4d-0bcd-522d-afb8-b88622571665" :question/math-academy-id "q-333863" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e51629-6667-5acc-bd6a-ec704ece3042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391320} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd7ad5a-34ab-514c-9a6a-8d2115767c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51e51629-6667-5acc-bd6a-ec704ece3042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391320} :answer-field/id #uuid "434ebb35-7b8e-549c-8b7b-fce5a160c319" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391319} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc03cba-b9cd-5b03-8c9f-3db38da3b8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391323} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1bdbd9-8ed8-52fc-9b43-ba91d49254b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{s