d ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))-(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f01e0fd-8dda-5963-bd62-3117a40e666b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{4x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391356} {:answer/id #uuid "b6836be3-cec9-57cb-a21b-071219a4dde5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391357} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf73d37-990c-579b-8541-cd063cdab67f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{4x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391358} {:answer/id #uuid "cc074290-e876-5085-881a-61d9e52b2247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391359} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac0d666-e590-5ce1-ad48-bdf383750c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{4x-C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bf73d37-990c-579b-8541-cd063cdab67f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{4x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391358} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b7d3d05-7562-5ef6-8b04-f9f26969b5da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7cb09257-d0fc-5dab-9c6e-cd5e26a7c67a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333901" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{y}^{2}-8{e}^{x}y+4{e}^{2x}+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{y}^{2}-8{e}^{x}y+4{e}^{2x}+{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=4,\\,b(x)=-8{e}^{x},\\,c(x)=4{e}^{2x}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)={e}^{x}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+{e}^{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+{e}^{x}=4(z+{e}^{x})^{2}-8{e}^{x}(z+{e}^{x})+4{e}^{2x}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+{e}^{x} & =4({e}^{2x}+2{e}^{x}z+{z}^{2})-8{e}^{x}z-8{e}^{2x}+4{e}^{2x}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {z}^{′}+{e}^{x} & =4{z}^{2}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =4{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =4x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{4x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-{e}^{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{4x+C} \\\\ y-{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{4x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4x+C}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{4x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91f4a159-0fa5-5fb3-9570-d38ee0ea271c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391363} {:answer/id #uuid "a0228ec7-3203-5716-9480-7f7ee6e6a922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391364} {:answer/id #uuid "de3cef7a-03a7-52ca-8479-785de1f38ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0228ec7-3203-5716-9480-7f7ee6e6a922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391364} :answer-field/id #uuid "09ebee1a-f1f5-5797-a0a3-8969a5355ef3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391362} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324be752-9b21-5deb-8fa1-85fe3b5d7305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391367} {:answer/id #uuid "841bec0d-a89f-517a-94be-65725c42d0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391368} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcbfa5f-091c-5415-9f8c-b0a117be08e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "841bec0d-a89f-517a-94be-65725c42d0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391368} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c4e2cbd-1be5-5824-8da3-b214d135bd35" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5191a0bf-606c-5508-9140-f8cbffb86988" :question/math-academy-id "q-333967" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-2x=0,\\,y(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3).$\n\n$$\ny(3)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(3)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2x-3{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2x-3{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3z}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=3.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3z}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-2$ and $z(1)=3.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2x-3z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 3}{{1}^{2}}=-7 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-7\\cdot 1=-7 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+3=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3-7=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-7$ | $-7$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2x-3z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-4)}{{2}^{2}}=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-4\\cdot 1=-4 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-4=-3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-4+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-7$ | $-7$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-3$\nand\n${y}^{′}(3)=z(3)≈0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72569b7e-dc1b-59a9-8d4a-ff9651c0478b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391372} {:answer/id #uuid "cfec4f1c-aec6-5e20-a91d-ade1522cd297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391373} {:answer/id #uuid "50416ef0-3c8a-5cc2-a0be-33930104acf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfec4f1c-aec6-5e20-a91d-ade1522cd297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391373} :answer-field/id #uuid "91774892-f177-5865-8240-6605b6d6bcb3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391371} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73fdbadc-25b5-5389-ae3f-6a97178bbfb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391376} {:answer/id #uuid "7f857271-83dd-5f04-8a00-56976c9c3a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391377} {:answer/id #uuid "89b22b6d-a6ba-5d10-80a2-23e54aa8b532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f857271-83dd-5f04-8a00-56976c9c3a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391377} :answer-field/id #uuid "4628b206-f875-5f30-94cb-025154de0b04" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45d8fb92-b013-52d1-8dce-907150e07f98" :question/math-academy-id "q-333994" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3).$\n\n$$\ny(3)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(3)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+2y & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =4{y}^{′}-2y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4{y}^{′}-2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4z-2y}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4z-2y}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4z-2y}{x}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{1}=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+6=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=7 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4z-2y}{x}=\\frac{4\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 0}{2}=14 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=7\\cdot 1=7 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=14\\cdot 1=14 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+7=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=7+14=21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $7$ | $7$ | $14$ | $14$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $21$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈7$\nand\n${y}^{′}(3)=z(3)≈21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e932bf7-b9db-535d-b1d7-2c47f0a471ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391381} {:answer/id #uuid "57f72f11-38e7-5f9a-8e91-eba04c0da69c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391382} {:answer/id #uuid "336353e1-0a96-58ce-b184-7f07b79e0da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391383} {:answer/id #uuid "02521657-c692-5efa-8419-f3916a74fb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391384} {:answer/id #uuid "29036cc4-573a-53e0-9b83-47ba2d610bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57f72f11-38e7-5f9a-8e91-eba04c0da69c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391382} :answer-field/id #uuid "14020034-4c7d-5a29-83f3-9d34befbad4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f26b8b6-ea22-55f9-adb9-e96204409f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-334053" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 10 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03626da-efdf-527f-904b-4a0094c28a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391388} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5b7f76-34fc-5589-a565-935bf344601a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391389} {:answer/id #uuid "56704239-e2fd-5c78-90ff-6b9bec163237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391390} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfa47fc-5498-5aea-b018-1c2589e2ea11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391391} {:answer/id #uuid "d30da62f-5264-58e4-8060-8a28c0ebc75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b03626da-efdf-527f-904b-4a0094c28a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391388} :answer-field/id #uuid "a866dd75-a93f-56b4-8102-f6bff27bb25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "048c0890-2cea-5141-b151-d83b632a790f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334058" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =0[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05af3055-c035-5e11-8946-8791c21a18ac" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Trapezoidal Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a65f23d9-ece1-5d94-9f1b-4fa3ff21de35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391395} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9d2a40-8db7-58a2-965b-5f8b9284015a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391396} {:answer/id #uuid "04c655f7-87ce-528a-8800-3f6394ecdb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391397} {:answer/id #uuid "f96ae150-da92-5738-ba1d-d4d1763de606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391398} {:answer/id #uuid "838f71c2-360e-538a-95d7-8b3b5dc7a597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f96ae150-da92-5738-ba1d-d4d1763de606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391398} :answer-field/id #uuid "df1f67de-316f-5c29-b742-1caa63eae0d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3e7b77a-8724-5c98-839a-9b13b32a04b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334077" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b04b62e0-4d73-59ef-8279-4fbd19989e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391402} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f49236-3a8d-5a10-8201-3bd22185b5d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391403} {:answer/id #uuid "e83af8b8-3e6f-5511-9762-b11e0353d127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391404} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec12f37-5132-52dc-8477-f4775fec044d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391405} {:answer/id #uuid "004feb22-2e9d-5fba-844f-78d0770b87e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e83af8b8-3e6f-5511-9762-b11e0353d127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391404} :answer-field/id #uuid "18366436-d621-5726-9326-142f72498a7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69d61c11-3667-5f2c-9c69-9cde3e0cb2a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-334078" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -16 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ -4{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =0[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c98219-1b0f-5781-b2c5-198ce54d0a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/87e39cbca1f6b5179f09a1526cf326de4ed205d9df5d0f86ee68585780e16a5f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391409} {:answer/id #uuid "0297f794-0ea5-57fe-bcc5-905675a7fa81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/63422efd3b1af838ee4bf6a7ac87e39694c2ed5e7b6931d16c70773f688abfbb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391410} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8e83fa-6135-5f10-be45-a6432675728e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/cee58e76fac807cd504037385e0b7c0b8aed1f726a3c6e010d127100d7274004.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391411} {:answer/id #uuid "35a99abb-b835-56ca-bd5c-d2ab7d53695b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4abb32b9a492e7d39b4504af7cf2a180e74dcca679804ed8198089cb68b7fb6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391412} {:answer/id #uuid "93efd4f4-2397-5a34-a956-28c23dc16c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5c/5c35d385e4ce7496a5fbbcbd344ce04583f7eaf8356e1a76a15625b77df75121.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93efd4f4-2397-5a34-a956-28c23dc16c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5c/5c35d385e4ce7496a5fbbcbd344ce04583f7eaf8356e1a76a15625b77df75121.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391413} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c297eab-ea3a-5828-82f2-d915c64d6374" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1d32c3a-674d-5f6b-9af5-31eda216d3b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334106" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/40/40b02cfc27ab7bc7bcc4a76523e106066b6ed6f56c184c7bd1f59a0cd1dcfc27.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5c/5c35d385e4ce7496a5fbbcbd344ce04583f7eaf8356e1a76a15625b77df75121.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec6a8eb8-c109-597e-96bb-9b561c34d9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391416} {:answer/id #uuid "f80fe0e9-1b8c-541a-b5cc-2a5606205e85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec6a8eb8-c109-597e-96bb-9b561c34d9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391416} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d21f3c6-f7fb-5b6c-8dd7-e35248a7f05f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391415} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4e740e-764f-5d22-86c6-d4dcab01dfc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391419} {:answer/id #uuid "a672958d-b9fd-537e-8f60-dcc5a9372518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391420} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a717c-b027-5816-86e7-e13b003665f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391421} {:answer/id #uuid "6a776299-946a-5a02-82fc-feb758ea43db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{x}^{2}-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a717c-b027-5816-86e7-e13b003665f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391421} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8d7bd14-76a5-5b01-9389-7f463996bec4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc71ef21-ab95-5a98-aab6-b8c717356264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391424} {:answer/id #uuid "38e92c9e-489f-5561-86aa-21233c9a65be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc71ef21-ab95-5a98-aab6-b8c717356264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391424} :answer-field/id #uuid "c322ded2-adc3-590e-9217-7eb66344715b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64f4cde4-f8ab-5629-93ea-f22c06ec9430" :question/math-academy-id "q-334108" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}-2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=10x-5{x}^{3},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}}, which is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ ({x}^{3}-2x)^{′} & (10x-5{x}^{3})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2 & 10-15{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-2x)(10-15{x}^{2})-(10x-5{x}^{3})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot 5(2-3{x}^{2})-5x(2-{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2})-5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x-(-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b182f484-befa-50e8-80e3-c6f147624f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391428} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad16653-3659-5bd7-a74c-cc62a1cf1c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ad16653-3659-5bd7-a74c-cc62a1cf1c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391429} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ef68bc-40c6-565a-8e8d-f4095e0889f2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391427} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "713dc9ce-04fd-5fa5-bfe7-8abc8ccbbcca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391431} {:answer/id #uuid "a56e147b-eda5-5939-8e23-ca63cfb432d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a56e147b-eda5-5939-8e23-ca63cfb432d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391432} :answer-field/id #uuid "d913da5f-b12a-58c7-b80e-1bbe27ab9c85" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f11b2387-2fc9-542d-8ba8-2598d7c3d41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391434} {:answer/id #uuid "de129c6c-ce47-5263-a095-78981544a286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391435} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e7fb56-bdf0-522f-9aaa-ecde39217ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391436} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b63844-1540-53e8-8fdf-1d085e57bd56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e7fb56-bdf0-522f-9aaa-ecde39217ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391436} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8be5cb-781b-53f4-bd01-09212a004cc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0932b3bc-4ddc-5e49-8937-50f983b26a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-334114" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′} & {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x-({cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124e7252-c219-570f-b100-c0298de29b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391440} {:answer/id #uuid "7047c9ba-6420-5ee3-83e3-011f1f7ed1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391441} {:answer/id #uuid "4daf1422-bb2d-5fa6-bbb6-425aa926474b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391442} {:answer/id #uuid "f5161c68-e970-54b3-8666-97c2da3f12a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391443} {:answer/id #uuid "a601d6b7-e919-549b-96e7-719507050536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7047c9ba-6420-5ee3-83e3-011f1f7ed1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391441} :answer-field/id #uuid "511785b1-2779-58b5-8f9d-56d17a54890e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "262f720c-cbdd-503c-9647-53ea02489b2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334117" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=3x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\,{y}^{2}-12{x}^{2}y+18{x}^{3}+3\n$$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\,{y}^{2}-12{x}^{2}y+18{x}^{3}+3,\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=2x,\\,b(x)=-12{x}^{2},\\,c(x)=18{x}^{3}+3.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=3x.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=3$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+3x\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+3.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+3=2x\\,(z+3x)^{2}-12{x}^{2}(z+3x)+18{x}^{3}+3.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+3 & =2x(9{x}^{2}+6xz+{z}^{2})-12{x}^{2}z-36{x}^{3}+18{x}^{3}+3 \\\\ {z}^{′}+3 & =2x{z}^{2}+3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-3x$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y-3x & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}+3x \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb23c291-a2fb-5e57-bbc9-7b2c02d0a1ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fd8ee38-cc19-57bf-a2d4-d938dacd9b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391447} {:answer/id #uuid "d579d267-d2ca-5a5b-8c7a-6f1279ca8058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391448} {:answer/id #uuid "cf548bcc-4e6b-52e3-9d1b-7b38aee23e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391449} {:answer/id #uuid "495953c3-2262-5b2d-9142-a607cd50c14f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391450} {:answer/id #uuid "c2974fed-872c-5d5d-b80b-97fa29f86a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "495953c3-2262-5b2d-9142-a607cd50c14f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8e2c428-19ec-50d1-94c6-9b91d9aba852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfdc15b3-f049-5c54-b917-bb040b11bf90" :question/math-academy-id "q-334122" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\,{y}^{2}-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,y+2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." :question/worked-solution "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\,{y}^{2}-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,y+2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=2x,\\,b(x)=-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,c(x)=2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2x\\,(z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,(z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2x({z}^{2}+2z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,(z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {z}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2x{z}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b84ae16b-ae44-5309-968e-7ca78cc49115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391454} {:answer/id #uuid "2ade99f4-d0aa-543e-9020-8962ed1d4988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391455} {:answer/id #uuid "eafefc36-d9fc-5bfb-ac57-f1ccc101175a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391456} {:answer/id #uuid "eaced104-c1ba-5aff-be45-2477dc3e739b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391457} {:answer/id #uuid "810ec073-1c10-5e62-97f8-9f8d05d07884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "810ec073-1c10-5e62-97f8-9f8d05d07884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391458} :answer-field/id #uuid "e169e90a-3a26-5b72-bd5a-f9439cfadfe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64c852b4-5cf9-5308-a3bf-3d121c7ebf57" :question/math-academy-id "q-334214" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}-[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21f5afe-e5c6-5ec3-abd8-3695510c29d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391461} {:answer/id #uuid "64b70de0-8654-5174-9f0a-e200a1f95f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391462} {:answer/id #uuid "91e455f0-fc59-529b-9915-a9c17975389f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391463} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0fc9f8-bda0-566f-8d40-c3e189c3533d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391464} {:answer/id #uuid "082e0e53-c474-57a8-8703-87fb40bfb432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64b70de0-8654-5174-9f0a-e200a1f95f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391462} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1ca386a-362b-5eef-b1b5-9eceaa2bfa06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb5e5230-b38a-5e0c-a077-5495818362fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334224" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(2+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}(3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}(3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-2[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+0[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b542bd66-f4f8-5b4e-a3e7-4717827c427a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9e6f595-8087-5dbd-8139-304da120ec34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unbounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391468} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4b344f-3032-50e7-811d-0221005ce729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "bounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd4b344f-3032-50e7-811d-0221005ce729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "bounded" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391469} :answer-field/id #uuid "aafde8dd-f176-57c0-adc4-2c7ebf5d023e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391467} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232eea7c-31f4-5544-b643-147187a62d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ellipse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391471} {:answer/id #uuid "a193c202-a97b-5b41-872a-fce67dcd5ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a spiral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "232eea7c-31f4-5544-b643-147187a62d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an ellipse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391471} :answer-field/id #uuid "d36b213e-45b1-50bc-990e-06b1564318ac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391470} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be8af45-e718-518c-ac49-5f84e3003a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391474} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd8486c-4c99-5d41-9b66-ab474ed67167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391475} {:answer/id #uuid "8de605b7-68ca-5322-ac4c-bfb0ccf0452d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8de605b7-68ca-5322-ac4c-bfb0ccf0452d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391476} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff9a3f2-06dd-524e-acfc-17eabe0d53d3" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391473} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6066ab8d-7398-5f44-8d31-be4c8b430716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "clockwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391478} {:answer/id #uuid "a513ded9-fc37-5828-8fbf-0affb2344799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "counterclockwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a513ded9-fc37-5828-8fbf-0affb2344799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "counterclockwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391479} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f3b7e68-3862-5d55-b31c-8ef864ba966e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ade9d10e-8ab5-5b95-969f-539a44434240" :question/math-academy-id "q-334273" :question/problem "![](images/28/2856c3b26abc3df90c228f4df3b95d9ee04a5c6cfd83cee78d1f6601c4bf8bdd.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i},$ where $a,b\\in R,$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8768dd56-c0fc-583e-822c-6575903b9078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220673} {:answer/id #uuid "89daedea-da9e-59f9-ad22-509d3847c15f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t}\\cos 2t & -e^{t}\\sin 2t \\\\ e^{t}\\sin 2t & e^{t}\\cos 2t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220674} {:answer/id #uuid "d66f2830-46a6-5121-889a-63b107df6397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos 2t & -\\sin 2t \\\\ \\sin 2t & \\cos 2t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220675} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed2e693-a120-58c3-95fc-d17cb9ff7c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}e^{t}\\cos t & e^{t}\\sin t \\\\ -e^{t}\\sin t & e^{t}\\cos t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220676} {:answer/id #uuid "7aebd347-1fac-5f4a-88d1-5def198f349e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}e\\cos 2 & -e\\sin 2 \\\\ e\\sin 2 & e\\cos 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220677}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7634ac73-0ede-5218-abe9-859f177734ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220672}] :question/id #uuid "ecd4b4f8-b1ae-5164-9bed-1e7df536d7e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334329" :question/problem "$x^{′} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x, x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos 2t \\\\ \\sin 2t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin 2t \\\\ \\cos 2t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$, shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f958ab6b-08af-5b23-82ee-08bd62f9162b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391481} {:answer/id #uuid "0829a874-481a-53dc-93a9-6db5f652920a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/7705e0976e796f3a5642d68287dcfef5315c6599f7e997b56a34ec534b89e76d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391482} {:answer/id #uuid "54edaaef-f58b-52a7-af21-0580f89bcb42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3aa0329f2b3017616c2010d4674c0dd13d9e277abe790e276a4e294087d6c685.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391483} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8952df-2170-5d6e-a565-913e80e05c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/568be19a1553bf837519d82e11f8ee391c7dce120605fc2855039eeff83ff1fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391484} {:answer/id #uuid "f41f25dd-037b-571c-8c29-af5ee91636ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dacab040d97838459bb9d0554ed08d09abd48f7716924fb3de2b52c1bf9cf556.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f41f25dd-037b-571c-8c29-af5ee91636ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dacab040d97838459bb9d0554ed08d09abd48f7716924fb3de2b52c1bf9cf556.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391485} :answer-field/id #uuid "c136d66c-4782-50c9-a536-134e39d5e737" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c4bce57-2231-51f6-b0eb-e664a1d75300" :question/math-academy-id "q-334385" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9988933a3f4393f745b1df6f0dc667a4074f5ee787fab43fb392b117368a8ca9.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/28/2809f28b49c9782db6fdf3f5a8be7fd99ec6d9a08db8e81d91c4d6bc53efcdda.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/7f/7fd67e8a768419dd497bc6345c5999ff921e6de492f60c5f217e93f8dc4d07c1.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/da/dacab040d97838459bb9d0554ed08d09abd48f7716924fb3de2b52c1bf9cf556.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2916d28-5efb-5314-8923-0573bec1151e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1dca375ebe6f8468f12a1729d697d8d35ff6b430a70e53639ab029254ac392fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391488} {:answer/id #uuid "ea635c52-25f9-5aa9-95df-3db5bd2c8c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/879cc00fedd506a0607bce3d3d36f99b489f599dadb6b13d4d1176fa24441853.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391489} {:answer/id #uuid "1757ec4c-4154-59ad-82df-49a519e58859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391490} {:answer/id #uuid "20b254ca-ca7c-577b-af73-4554ed77ae3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9a56a6a1f17d58226fb2fac905247b339be111335637a15d3e8c447aab3f3a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391491} {:answer/id #uuid "de04ae64-037b-57a5-a073-94201161c12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1757ec4c-4154-59ad-82df-49a519e58859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391490} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b27342d-f535-5330-acd6-ae18222b5178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb0146a-edb8-5224-b29c-36d6ad8aec81" :question/math-academy-id "q-334532" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c3afc4bedb411c778773190a3b9bc1db0b376dd69592ba744e8225ec900cc45.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-1 \\\\ y=2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/0d/0d506993d3bfbb727dffd44ced1a588b40e2fefa86e7f2780d90277c9d0a9eed.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+1 \\\\ y=-2t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e8/e88dfa02c57a5c31df14fdf6f963f7d568eaebccbbbfba6d8f2958593ac4693a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "731b0949-2836-51f7-a241-1c29cd44179b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391495} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b137cf-d2c6-5334-b289-2da9c0b93f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.257" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391496} {:answer/id #uuid "aed549b4-cfd0-5727-ab07-254a87731612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391497} {:answer/id #uuid "080d635e-2b40-51b4-97d2-08f53f6fae60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.181" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391498} {:answer/id #uuid "03671609-7538-5e97-ac49-095e671af69c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.094" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "731b0949-2836-51f7-a241-1c29cd44179b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391495} :answer-field/id #uuid "651a4ec8-2dbb-5846-aa30-211a9e7851fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6671ce6c-52d6-5c82-9895-663201543b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391501} {:answer/id #uuid "15694831-cfd0-55f4-8a3c-a91a22206a3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36.000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391502} {:answer/id #uuid "33982efd-1cd9-5bc2-a5ff-34aa92a42331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63.000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391503} {:answer/id #uuid "32e831fc-3b7e-5729-ae17-30b540211b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81.000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391504} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4bce46-7707-5ea0-8b4a-310ef21cefbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33982efd-1cd9-5bc2-a5ff-34aa92a42331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63.000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391503} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0909f09-0745-5b2d-9f8a-2e4f162268c0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391500} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ad6941-f940-5d85-9fda-ce1bd90ee15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391507} {:answer/id #uuid "a1824d06-c84e-561e-bfa1-68a2ef8af832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.257" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391508} {:answer/id #uuid "bc576189-d0ec-5430-82c4-aba63b34d03f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391509} {:answer/id #uuid "fd30b658-9405-51f2-a9ee-b73d1ba07b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.181" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391510} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fcafdb-a89e-53fe-97bf-b072c2144a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.094" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd30b658-9405-51f2-a9ee-b73d1ba07b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.181" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391510} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a404af0-1b18-5aba-9b08-6fb4793a95b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391506} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448586e8-eb26-5489-beb1-ca113551f227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391513} {:answer/id #uuid "718d1144-6bc4-51a5-b7a9-03d96e8cd290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391514} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b20284-e318-55cd-bfd3-13493f883312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391515} {:answer/id #uuid "de729150-01d6-5f51-9aff-702963293c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391516} {:answer/id #uuid "83c114ba-a0a8-593b-84b8-95a0ae1fe8d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de729150-01d6-5f51-9aff-702963293c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391516} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f0e7e9-24c6-586e-b716-02d194a97ed6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db1b2cc2-9ed0-52dd-b570-f28962fe5ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-334567" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =2(0.6)^{4}-(0.6)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.129\\,6-1\\cdot 0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.259\\,2-0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.181\\,44 \\\\  & ≈0.181.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =2(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.003\\,2-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.002\\,88 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.181\\,44$ to $0.002\\,88.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.181\\,44}{0.002\\,88}=63≈63.000.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈63 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(63) \\\\ p & ≈3.771….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d5d9391-7982-58dd-a645-5c99a26ba1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1dca375ebe6f8468f12a1729d697d8d35ff6b430a70e53639ab029254ac392fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391520} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fb7b9d-bce2-5833-ba39-dee6740b6f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9a56a6a1f17d58226fb2fac905247b339be111335637a15d3e8c447aab3f3a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391521} {:answer/id #uuid "e9563d1d-6cb6-56a5-b967-eb8053b98509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391522} {:answer/id #uuid "fc955bd4-2195-513e-a08f-869442249642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/41a70a3485c8b5000c832ac5a9d443c8abb576d6d60cb0391b2cc4cf2cae60c0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391523} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e333d0-2d0c-5b1c-b0b8-33ca89e4c220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/69/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9563d1d-6cb6-56a5-b967-eb8053b98509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391522} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd1cf8cc-b7e6-52f0-80be-bd0217b78f76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c07d6a46-4ba6-5448-9f13-79808c35273d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334639" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/60/6044d391016e37af929b36e89e0a802151b5710707cc510e9e7f70bf9a2d1c0d.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-2 \\\\ y=2t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/54/54bdfaf14b4f580e1476d4649e03cd71cb8203e23ae318daa65251fd7ce02ba1.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+2 \\\\ y=-2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/ed/edb5558ed7b5ee0e4aacb4eb67d74747fcc366f1508e16b3e99f3e0d5ee53306.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/10/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "971de907-aa22-5e54-86e1-730856500bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155357} {:answer/id #uuid "adeb3a38-8e02-56f1-833f-d049b423ba2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155358} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d323a2-e647-521b-99a9-c99cacd126af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155359} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ad2711-3588-50f5-80fa-45a5f56319fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155360} {:answer/id #uuid "38fa238c-1f65-57b3-9eaa-dde23ccc163d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155361}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5c3ccbab-e46c-5e80-aa80-cb21b6d4a36f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "999fcf21-87bd-512b-8863-b0ee5326264c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L2$ and $L3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155363} {:answer/id #uuid "28d1e7ce-5ad8-501e-9daa-7ba420158f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L1$ and $L3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155364}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5d5cbe32-8cd0-5e0b-b661-19fde6427bac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155362} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d4ebb7c-62d1-58fa-a8c9-d2e9996cdea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155366} {:answer/id #uuid "ded68a95-0a78-59ea-bb21-e9cfc20deb9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155367} {:answer/id #uuid "0dceb9f9-6fe6-5e84-be8b-c8b97a20a7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155368} {:answer/id #uuid "3e78c090-60be-5986-9ec9-f7914a293d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155369} {:answer/id #uuid "46f04aef-bce5-5182-bec8-b656dc8495c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155370}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f60f26d3-d734-5423-9bae-c9cd95e8d1ec" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155365} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c83ae94d-8aa1-56d6-bb5d-258be8eeaed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155372} {:answer/id #uuid "e43b51bb-1cd8-55ae-a203-edf7630b8523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155373} {:answer/id #uuid "16afd9e4-afc2-5f01-a536-d2255c8fbfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155374} {:answer/id #uuid "6b26beea-fddf-5b32-84c2-afd8ec818462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155375} {:answer/id #uuid "76d81dc6-7bc3-51f1-872e-d15ba95a6c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155376}] :answer-field/id #uuid "27d6a495-8e1a-5be4-a327-1398919f82b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155371} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a9c71c6-9f45-5aa3-b957-5b9d98cc8bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155378} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8b226d-9e25-5fd9-ac6d-861dc4c50e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155379} {:answer/id #uuid "7268eed5-cd68-5ed5-99a0-d0af2f4f1a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155380}] :answer-field/id #uuid "005e5191-da7d-5185-ac3c-67cc478128b1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155377}] :question/id #uuid "189a55f5-72ee-590d-b213-1f6bb96fbc77" :question/math-academy-id "q-334685" :question/problem "Consider the following statement.\n\n> If $f(x) = 3e^{2x} + 2x$, then $f = O(e^{2x})$ as $x → ∞$.\n\nA proof of the statement is given below\n\n> $L1:f = O(3e^{2x} + 2x)$\n> $L2:lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{2x}{3e^{2x}}) = 0$\n> $L3:2x = O(3e^{2x})$\n> $L4:f = O(3e^{2x})$\n> $L5:f = O(e^{2x})$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L1$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from lines\n{{field-2}} and\n{{field-3}}.\n\nLine $L5$ follows from line {{field-4}} and\n{{field-5}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9c2220-030e-5418-915c-e34d7962c077" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing a Solution of the Lotka-Volterra Model Given the Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90980cfd-c2a9-5638-b772-14f9a957f705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164602} {:answer/id #uuid "1a92706f-1a2c-5944-b227-27df5e3ec74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164603}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e771e7-f906-5919-9acb-50d46d1c60f0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bd7fc13-4be5-50a3-877b-3c4f8f830f14" :answer/type